#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183996 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3155a85f-7bd8-584c-a5ae-e2e18dd60cd2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the First Quadrant"}] :question/id #uuid "c049aaef-f59a-5113-b739-20cc7a6d802c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3520" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v$, we have\n\n$\\sin u = \\dfrac{3}{4}, \\qquad \\cos u = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}, \\qquad \\cos v = \\dfrac{4}{5}$.\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, and\n$\\sin\\left(u + v\\right) = \\dfrac{a- b\\sqrt 7}{20}$,\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b$." :question/worked-solution "First, recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u+v) = \\sin u \\cos v+ \\cos u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{u}$, $\\cos{u}$, and $\\cos{v}$. To find the missing value of $\\sin{v}$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 v + \\cos^2 v= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\sin^2 v = 1- \\cos^2 v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin^{2} v &= 1 - \\cos^{2} v \\\\\n&= 1 - (\\frac{4}{5})^{2} \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{4^{2}}{5^{2}} \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{16}{25} \\\\\n&= \\frac{25}{25} - \\frac{16}{25} \\\\\n&= \\frac{25 - 16}{25} \\\\\n&= \\frac{9}{25}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin v &= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{3}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\sin v= \\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin (u + v) &= \\sin u\\cos v + \\cos u\\sin v \\\\\n&= (\\frac{3}{4})(\\frac{4}{5}) + (-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4})(\\frac{3}{5}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{12}{20} - \\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{20} \\\\\n&= \\frac{12 - 3\\sqrt{7}}{20}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=12, b=3$, and\n\n$$\na+b = 15\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184076 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "99d7256f-6130-52b4-8188-3b1388c63f2b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the Second, Third, or Fourth Quadrants"}] :question/id #uuid "06df772e-eb43-55e8-9ede-3fc2f7ae291d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9984" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v$, we have\n$\\cos u = \\dfrac{7}{25}, \\qquad \\sin v = \\dfrac{4}{5}, \\qquad \\cos v = \\dfrac {3} {5}$.\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the fourth quadrant, find the value of $\\sin(u-v)$." :question/worked-solution "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u-v) = \\sin u \\cos v- \\cos u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\cos{u}$, $\\sin{v}$, and $\\cos{v}$. To find the missing value of $\\sin{u}$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 u + \\cos^2 u= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\sin^2 u = 1- \\cos^2 u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin^{2} u &= 1 - \\cos^{2} u \\\\\n&= 1 - (\\frac{7}{25})^{2} \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{7^{2}}{25^{2}} \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{49}{625} \\\\\n&= \\frac{625}{625} - \\frac{49}{625} \\\\\n&= \\frac{576}{625}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin u &= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{576}{625}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{24}{25}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the fourth quadrant, where sine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\sin u= -\\dfrac{24}{25}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\sin(u - v) &= \\sin u \\cos v - \\cos u \\sin v \\\\[5pt] &= \\left(-\\dfrac {24}{25} \\right)\\left(\\dfrac {3} {5}\\right) - \\left(\\dfrac {7}{25}\\right) \\left(\\dfrac {4} {5}\\right) \\\\[5pt] &= -\\dfrac {72} {125} - \\dfrac {28} {125} \\\\[5pt] &= -\\dfrac {100} {125} \\\\[5pt] &= -\\dfrac {4} {5}. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184396 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "99d7256f-6130-52b4-8188-3b1388c63f2b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the Second, Third, or Fourth Quadrants"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d540adb0-4822-5764-8a8a-d96c8e4c2e9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186180826} {:answer/id #uuid "03888c6d-eae5-5e8c-a2b1-1b9b021a205c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186180827} {:answer/id #uuid "0995b034-c51a-560f-9e1e-befb26b7f1f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186180828} {:answer/id #uuid "34f6a551-a30b-5bdb-bc46-03ba79c9bfd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186180829} {:answer/id #uuid "44f85a2f-1097-5205-93f2-92cfdb168e55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186180830}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a24e2409-8ab5-5ff2-b172-35006aeb5209" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186180825}] :question/id #uuid "ba92bee5-a4eb-55dd-b31e-91c198d4e5df" :question/math-academy-id "q-119179" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v$, we have\n$\\sin u =-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}, \\cos u = \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{4}, \\cos v =-\\frac{5}{6}$.\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the second quadrant, and\n$\\sin (u - v) = \\frac{a\\sqrt{5} - b}{24}$,\n\ncalculate the value of $a + b$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184768 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "99d7256f-6130-52b4-8188-3b1388c63f2b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the Second, Third, or Fourth Quadrants"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a813978b-8f19-5e5d-abd2-a93aef026126" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{33}{65}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183498} {:answer/id #uuid "f2ae1ea7-3a94-53d0-9b09-98c9caefdad1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{49}{65}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183499} {:answer/id #uuid "35d271db-2267-5e22-af58-01253fbc426e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{63}{65}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183500} {:answer/id #uuid "496dfba6-4cb0-5d57-b8dc-d7cdcbc3cb76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{65}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183501} {:answer/id #uuid "73aa38c8-822b-509f-8d20-efed18896dec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{23}{65}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183502}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f590cb36-f031-5022-9995-4ed0999d016d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183497}] :question/id #uuid "4aa47f46-244d-51e1-8773-d950dc59e732" :question/math-academy-id "q-65423" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v$, we have\n$\\cos x =-\\frac{4}{5}, \\sin y = \\frac{12}{13}, \\cos y = \\frac{5}{13}$.\n\nGiven that $x$ lies in the second quadrant, find the value of $\\sin (y - x)$."}]}
